1
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:10,686
Deep dive: pricing. What the app prices, how it splits a sheet’s cost, and how to build a full quote from its numbers. Example prices throughout.

2
00:00:10,686 --> 00:00:20,922
Prices live on each material in the Library. Open the white melamine.

3
00:00:20,922 --> 00:00:34,680
Costed by area, $2.85 per square foot. A 5 x 12 ft sheet is 60 ft², so one sheet costs $171. Price per ft² keeps working when the sheet size changes.

4
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:43,767
Density is only a label; nothing here touches cut sizes. Cancel. The other boards were priced the same way in Real jobs u06.

5
00:00:43,767 --> 00:01:03,604
Back in the job, Nesting. Area and cost splits every sheet into three: the parts, remnants worth keeping, and scrap.

6
00:01:03,604 --> 00:01:16,987
Sheet 1, the 1/4" HDF backs: $51.01 a sheet. The 13 parts use 81.8% of it ($41.71); the rest is scrap ($9.30). You pay for the whole sheet either way.

7
00:01:16,987 --> 00:01:31,939
Sheet 4, melamine, 93% used: the better the nest, the less of each sheet is scrap. That is why spacing and rotation settings matter to price.

8
00:01:31,939 --> 00:01:47,671
Sheet 6 leaves big offcuts. Remnants kept have their own line: material you still own, and can use on the next job.

9
00:01:47,671 --> 00:02:02,352
The job line: six sheets cost $825.09. Less the remnants you keep, the job uses $625.75. Quote the first to be safe, the second to be sharp.

10
00:02:02,352 --> 00:02:26,230
Per part: Areas and costs CSV gives each part its share of its sheet. Useful for pricing one cabinet, or a change order.

11
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:36,529
What the sheet price leaves out: edgeband, hardware and labour. Their quantities are all in the BOM CSV.

12
00:02:36,529 --> 00:02:51,363
Worked example: 157 ft of 1 mm ABS at $0.25/ft is about $39; 16 Salice hinges and plates at $6 a pair, $96; 6 pairs of TANDEMs at $45, $270.

13
00:02:51,363 --> 00:03:04,506
So boards $825 + edgeband ~$40 + hardware ~$400 (with screws, pins and dowels) is about $1,265 in materials, before labour and margin.

14
00:03:04,506 --> 00:03:15,432
Labour is yours to add: machine time from the programs, plus banding, assembly and install. The app gives you every quantity; you set the rates.

15
00:03:15,432 --> 00:03:21,677
So: price boards per ft², nest well to cut scrap, decide how to treat remnants, and add edgeband, hardware and labour from the BOM.
